       }
      ],
      "text": "They may be using **different scales or averaging periods**. If by PSI you mean Singapore’s NEA index, its **24-hour PSI is “Moderate” at 51–100 and “Unhealthy” at 101–200**. An app may instead report a different AQI, use a shorter-term PM₂.₅ reading, or pull data from another location—so its label can be worse even when PSI is moderate. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block&utm_source=openai))\n\nCheck that both readings are for the **same place, time, pollutant, and averaging period**. Also, “unhealthy” thresholds vary between indexes; for example, the U.S. AQI calls **151–200 “Unhealthy.”** ([airnow.gov](https://www.airnow.gov/aqi/aqi-basics//?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you share your location, the app name, and the two numbers, I can help compare them.",
